{"cuisine/colombian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/colombian-cuisine","n":"Colombian cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Colombia","intro":[["Colombian cuisine is a culinary tradition of six main regions within Colombia: Insular, Caribbean, Pacific, Andean, Orinoco, and Amazonian. Colombian cuisine varies regionally and is influenced by Indigenous Colombian, Spanish, and African cuisines, with a slight Arab influence in some regions."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"colombian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colombian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":5,"links":[["Arepa","dish/arepa"],["Calentao","dish/calentao"],["Cholado","dish/cholado"],["Pandebono","dish/pandebono"],["Puchero","dish/puchero"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"My Colombian Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mycolombianrecipes.com","note":"Medellín-born Erica Dinho's blog of traditional Colombian recipes.","url":"https://www.mycolombianrecipes.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Colombiana: A Rediscovery of Recipes and Rituals from the Soul of Colombia","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Mariana Velásquez, 2021","note":"Bogotá-born food stylist's tour of Colombian regional cooking.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780063019430","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780063019430"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Maize flour","ingredient/maize-flour"],["Maize meal","ingredient/maize-meal"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Cassava","ingredient/cassava"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Corn starch","ingredient/corn-starch"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Starch","ingredient/starch"],["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Pasta","ingredient/pasta"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Turnip","ingredient/turnip"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within Colombian cooking","count":1,"links":[["Muisca cuisine","cuisine/muisca-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Colombia","count":1,"links":[["Wayuu foodways","cuisine/wayuu-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d7/Ajiaco.jpg/500px-Ajiaco.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Mauricio Giraldo from Bogotá, Colombia","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ajiaco.jpg"}},"cuisine/ou-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/ou-cuisine","n":"Ou cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Ou cuisine, alternatively known as Wenzhou cuisine, is one of the four schools of Zhejiang cuisine, whereas Zhejiang cuisine is one of the eight major cuisines in China. The ancient name of Wenzhou is Dong'ou. After the founding of the People’s Republic of China, in order to improve the popularity of Wenzhou cuisine, catering experts has renamed the cuisine with its ancient name, \"Ou cai\". The major elements in Ou cuisine are seafood, which tastes fresh, light, and not bland. During the cooking process, it emphasizes \"two light and a heavy,\" that is, light oil, light thickening, and heavy knife action, and it has a noticeable distinctive style."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ou-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ou_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/a/af/Wenzhou_glutinous_rice.jpg","w":500,"h":335,"by":"LindaZ03","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/